consiSt("la",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Louisiana","d":"Court of last resort. Reviews court of appeal judgments mainly by discretionary writ (certiorari) under Supreme Court Rule X, which lists the considerations that ordinarily must be present for a grant (conflicting decisions, significant unresolved issues, erroneous application of law causing material injustice, and so on). Regulates bar admission, pro hac vice (Rule XVII § 13), lawyer discipline (Rule XIX, through the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board), and e-filing in its own court (Rule XLII, the CDX system)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Courts of Appeal (First through Fifth Circuits)","d":"Five circuit courts of appeal hear appeals from district, family, juvenile, city, and parish courts within their circuits and exercise supervisory jurisdiction by writ over interlocutory rulings. All five follow the Uniform Rules of Louisiana Courts of Appeal (version with amendments effective July 1, 2026), and each also has local rules (for example, on paper copies and e-filing)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts (including Civil District Court for the Parish of Orleans)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized by judicial district (one or more parishes). Civil practice is governed by the Code of Civil Procedure and by the statewide Rules for Louisiana District Courts (Title I general rules, Title II civil rules), plus district-by-district appendices. Cases are randomly allotted to a division (C.C.P. art. 253.1)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Family and Juvenile Courts","d":"District Court Rules Titles IV and V refer to family courts and juvenile courts as well as district courts; where no separate court exists, district courts hear these matters. Not separately researched."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"City Courts, Parish Courts, and Justice of the Peace Courts","d":"Book VIII of the Code of Civil Procedure (arts. 4831–5003) governs courts of limited jurisdiction. Parish courts share jurisdiction with district courts up to $20,000 in dispute (art. 4842); city courts generally up to $15,000, with higher limits for city courts listed in art. 4843(D) and beyond. Jury trials are prohibited in parish, city, and justice of the peace courts (art. 4871); a defendant entitled to a jury may transfer the case to district court (art. 4872). City and parish court appeals go to the court of appeal within 10 days (arts. 5001–5002); justice of the peace appeals are by trial de novo in the parish or district court (arts. 4924–4925)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Louisiana has an anti-SLAPP procedure: the special motion to strike, La. C.C.P. art. 971","d":"Scope: a cause of action against a person arising from any act in furtherance of that person’s right of petition or free speech under the U.S. or Louisiana Constitution in connection with a public issue. Covered acts include statements before, or in connection with an issue under review by, a legislative, executive, or judicial body or other official proceeding; statements in a place open to the public or a public forum on an issue of public interest; and any other conduct furthering petition or speech rights on a public issue or issue of public interest.\nStandard: the motion is granted unless the court determines that the plaintiff has established a probability of success on the claim. The court considers the pleadings and supporting and opposing affidavits. A finding that the plaintiff showed a probability of success is admissible later in the case.\nThe article does not apply to enforcement actions by the attorney general, a district attorney, or a city attorney acting as public prosecutor. “Petition” and “plaintiff” include a reconventional demand and a plaintiff in reconvention, so counterclaims are covered.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 971(A), (E), (F)","v":true},{"t":"Deadline: 90 days from service; hearing within 30 days; discovery stayed","d":"File within 90 days of service of the petition, or later in the court’s discretion on terms it sets. If the plaintiff voluntarily dismisses before the answer delay runs, the defendant keeps the right to file the motion within the same 90 days, and it is heard under art. 971.\nThe motion is to be noticed for hearing not more than 30 days after service unless docket conditions require a later hearing.\nAll discovery is stayed when notice of the motion is filed, until notice of entry of the order ruling on it; the court may allow specified discovery on noticed motion for good cause.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 971(C)(1)–(3), (D)","v":true},{"t":"Fees go to the prevailing party, either side","d":"A prevailing party on a special motion to strike “shall be awarded” reasonable attorney fees and costs. The text is not limited to a prevailing defendant, so a plaintiff who defeats the motion can also recover.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 971(B)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: no special appeal rule in the current article","d":"The current text of art. 971 (last amended by Acts 2012, No. 449) contains no provision of its own on appeal. Whether a ruling is appealable therefore turns on the general rules: a judgment granting the motion and dismissing the claims is a final judgment (or a partial final judgment needing designation under art. 1915(B) if claims remain), while a denial is interlocutory and is reviewed by supervisory writ under art. 2083(C). Confirm with current case law before relying on this.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 971, 1915, 2083 (appeal route is Consi’s inference from the text)","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: apply to the Disciplinary Board first, then move the court","d":"Step 1: the out-of-state lawyer files the Application for Pro Hac Vice Admission (form in Part C of the Rule XVII Appendix) with the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board, verified by both the out-of-state lawyer and Louisiana counsel, with the fee. Disciplinary Counsel issues a letter approving or disapproving it (with reasons if disapproved).\nStep 2: the Louisiana lawyer files a written ex parte motion in the court or agency where the case is pending, attaching the Disciplinary Counsel letter, served on all parties who have appeared (and on Disciplinary Counsel if disapproved), with proof of service.\nObjections: Disciplinary Counsel or any party may object within 20 days of service, with a verified affidavit giving a factual basis, and may ask for a hearing or move to withdraw an admission already granted.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(3)(i)–(iii), (A)(4)(i)","v":true},{"t":"Fee: $450 to the Disciplinary Board, plus any court fee","d":"A non-refundable $450.00 fee is paid to the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board, separate from any fee the court or agency charges. No fee if the lawyer will not charge the client and works with a pro bono project or nonprofit legal services organization in a civil case for its clients, or is in a criminal or habeas case for an indigent defendant.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(4)(ii)–(iii)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel is mandatory and stays responsible","d":"Eligibility requires acting in association with a Louisiana-licensed lawyer as required by La. R.S. 37:214. The in-state lawyer remains responsible to the client and for the conduct of the proceeding, and must tell the client when that lawyer’s independent judgment differs from the out-of-state lawyer’s.\nEligible lawyers: members in good standing of the bar of any U.S. District Court or of the highest court of any state, territory, or D.C., who (a) practice solely for an employer and its affiliates, (b) neither reside nor are regularly employed at an office in Louisiana, or (c) reside in Louisiana but practice from offices in other states and only temporarily here.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(1)(i)–(ii), (A)(2)(iii); La. R.S. 37:214 (not collected)","v":true},{"t":"The 30-day-before-trial cutoff and the ‘regular practice’ ground","d":"Absent extraordinary circumstances fully explained in the motion, courts and agencies will not entertain a pro hac vice motion filed within 30 days of the scheduled trial or hearing date.\nA motion ordinarily should be granted unless the court finds reason to believe, among other things, that the applicant’s appearances are so frequent as to constitute regular practice in Louisiana, that admission would harm the administration of justice or other parties’ legitimate interests, or that the applicant is not competent or ethically fit. No numeric cap on admissions appears in § 13. Admission can be revoked for those reasons or any other reason the court deems appropriate.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(3)(ii), (iv)–(v)","v":true},{"t":"Work allowed without pro hac vice; arbitration and mediation","d":"Without admission, an out-of-state lawyer may consult with Louisiana lawyers and potential clients, prepare for a potential Louisiana proceeding if the lawyer reasonably believes they are eligible for pro hac vice, work on out-of-state proceedings, and prepare for and take part in any ADR proceeding (arbitration, mediation) wherever held. These services are not unauthorized practice even if no case is filed or admission is denied. The lawyer may not solicit or advertise in publications directed solely to Louisiana, and may practice here only temporarily.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(B)(1)–(8), (C); La. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice lawyers are subject to Louisiana discipline and court rules","d":"From the application on, the out-of-state lawyer submits to the authority of Louisiana courts and the Disciplinary Board for all conduct relating to the proceeding, under Supreme Court Rule XIX, the Rules of Professional Conduct, contempt and sanctions orders, local rules, and court policies, and must become familiar with them.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(5)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances in district court: Notice of Limited Appearance","d":"A Louisiana lawyer may enroll for a limited purpose by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance (Appendix 9.12A for family law, 9.12B for other cases) stating the limitation by subject matter, proceeding, date, or time period under Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2(c). It is signed by lawyer and client; if the client cannot sign at filing, a client-signed certificate of the scope must be filed within 10 days. Every pleading filed under it must state in bold on the signature page: “Attorney for limited purpose of [matter or proceeding].” The rule’s comment says pro hac vice lawyers must comply with Rule XVII § 13.","c":"La. Dist. Ct. R. 9.12 and comments","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: Supreme Court CDX is for Louisiana-admitted lawyers; lower courts vary","d":"Supreme Court e-filing through the Court Data/Document Exchange (cdx.lasc.org) is open to attorneys admitted to and in good standing with the Supreme Court of Louisiana who complete mandatory training; filings must be text-searchable PDF/A. A filing completed before midnight Central Time on the due date is timely, though filings after 4:30 p.m. are processed the next business day. A rejected filing may be re-filed within 7 calendar days and keeps its original filing time.\nCourts of appeal accept e-filing only if permitted by their local rules (Uniform Rule 2-13), and district courts follow their clerks’ systems; neither was separately researched. A pro hac vice lawyer will normally file through Louisiana counsel.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XLII, §§ 2–4, 6, 7; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-13","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Louisiana Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Adopted by the Supreme Court and published by the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board. The collected compilation is current “with amendments through May 10, 2023” (the Rule 1.10 order); the Supreme Court’s list of rule-change orders through November 2026 shows no later amendment to these Rules. The Rules track the ABA Model Rules in structure, with the Louisiana differences below. Lawyer discipline is under Supreme Court Rule XIX.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct (LADB compilation, amendments through May 10, 2023); La. Sup. Ct. rule-change orders list","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.8(e): limited financial help to necessitous clients is allowed","d":"Unlike the ABA rule, Louisiana lets a lawyer give a client in necessitous circumstances minimal financial assistance for documented subsistence needs (food, shelter, utilities, insurance, non-litigation medical care, transportation, education), if without it the client could not start or keep the case going. Limits: no offer before being hired, no use as an inducement, no advertising it, no interest or fees on the lawyer’s own funds, and third-party loan interest passed on is capped. Overhead may not be passed to clients; listed costs (computer research, copying, postage, mileage) may be charged at actual cost with informed consent.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e)(1)–(5)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.5: written contingency contract and detailed fee-in-advance rules","d":"A contingent fee agreement must be in a writing signed by the client, with a copy given at signing, stating the percentage on settlement, trial, or appeal, the expenses deducted, and whether before or after the fee. Fee splitting with another firm requires the client’s written agreement, written disclosure of each lawyer’s share, and meaningful legal services by each lawyer (not ABA’s ‘proportion of services or joint responsibility’). Rule 1.5(f) sorts advance payments (general retainer, fixed or minimum fee, advance deposit) into operating or trust accounts, and requires immediate refund of the undisputed unearned portion in a fee dispute, with the disputed portion held in trust.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(c), (e), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.10: screening, but not when the switching lawyer was on the other side of a pending case","d":"Louisiana allows screening of a lateral lawyer to avoid imputed disqualification, with written notice to the former client, but not if the lawyer represented the former client in an adjudicative proceeding directly adverse to a current firm client and that proceeding is still pending when the lawyer changes firms.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(c)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 8.4: no ABA 8.4(g) discrimination rule; bar on threatening charges instead","d":"Louisiana’s Rule 8.4 does not contain the ABA’s 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Its 8.4(g) instead makes it misconduct to threaten to present criminal or disciplinary charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(a)–(g)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising: file ads with the LSBA; detailed Rules 7.1–7.10","d":"Louisiana has its own advertising regime. Subject to Rule 7.8 exemptions, a lawyer who advertises in public media or sends unsolicited written communications must file a copy of each with the LSBA Rules of Professional Conduct Committee before or at first dissemination, with the required information and fee, and gets a filing number; advance review at least 30 days before dissemination is optional. Out-of-state lawyers who advertise to Louisiana residents should read Rules 7.2 and 7.4–7.9 closely.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 7.7(b)–(d), 7.8","v":true},{"t":"Rule 5.5(e): do not hire disbarred or suspended lawyers","d":"A Louisiana addition: a lawyer may not employ or engage, in connection with the practice of law, a person the lawyer knows or should know is disbarred or permanently resigned in lieu of discipline, and may employ a suspended or disability-inactive lawyer only under conditions set out in the rule. Rule 5.5(d)(1) in-house practice requires a limited license under Supreme Court Rule XVII § 14.","c":"La. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(d)(1), (e)","v":true}],"style":"State of Louisiana","home":"https://www.lasc.org/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"No motion to dismiss: plead the right exception, at the right time, or lose it","d":"Rule 12-style defenses are raised by exception. Declinatory (insufficient citation or service, including failure to request service within 90 days; lis pendens; improper venue; lack of personal jurisdiction) and dilatory exceptions (prematurity, vagueness, improper cumulation, lack of capacity, and others) must be filed together, before or in the answer and before any other pleading seeking relief, and before a default is signed. Objections not pleaded are waived, including venue. The peremptory exception (prescription, no cause of action, no right of action, res judicata, subject-matter jurisdiction, and others) can be filed until submission for decision, and no evidence is allowed on no cause of action.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 44(C), 922, 925–928, 931"},{"t":"Abandonment: three years without a step ends the case automatically","d":"A suit is abandoned, without any order, if no party takes a step in the trial court for three years; formal discovery served on a party, filed or not, counts. A 2026 amendment (Acts 2026, No. 127) requires the mover’s affidavit to list record filings for the prior three years and written communications in the final year, and sets 30 days from sheriff’s service to move to set aside and 60 days to appeal. Abandonment also wipes out the interruption of prescription the suit created (Civil Code art. 3463(B)), except that dismissal of a suit on a conventional obligation still interrupts prescription under art. 561(L). Calendar a step at least every two and a half years.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 561; La. Civ. Code art. 3463(B)"},{"t":"Prescription: tort claims now two years, but check the old one-year rule for older injuries","d":"Acts 2024, No. 423 repealed Civil Code arts. 3492 and 3493 effective July 1, 2024 and enacted art. 3493.1: delictual actions prescribe in two years from the day injury or damage is sustained (with a special start for damage to immovables in art. 3493.2). Claims arising before July 1, 2024 may still be governed by the former one-year period; the Act’s transition provision was not collected, so confirm it before relying on two years for older claims. Prescription must be specially pleaded; the court will not raise it except in defaults. Filing in an incompetent court or improper venue interrupts prescription only as to defendants served within the period (with a 7-day suspension for a competent court of improper venue, added in 2025).","c":"La. Civ. Code arts. 3462, 3493.1, 3493.2; Acts 2024, No. 423; La. C.C.P. art. 927(B)"},{"t":"Request service within 90 days, and pay the clerk","d":"Service of citation must be requested on every named defendant within 90 days of filing (or of an amended petition adding the defendant), and the clerk’s service fees must be paid within that period (or 30 days after a statement of additional fees). A defendant can obtain dismissal without prejudice by declinatory exception or contradictory motion unless good cause is shown, and voluntary or involuntary dismissal can erase the interruption of prescription. Service is by the sheriff; a private process server requires a court appointment after the sheriff fails for 10 days.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1201(C)–(E), 1293, 1672(C); La. Civ. Code art. 3463(B)"},{"t":"Devolutive vs. suspensive appeal: 60 days or 30 days plus bond","d":"You appeal by obtaining an order of appeal from the trial court, not by filing a notice. A devolutive appeal (no stay, no bond) is due within 60 days, and a suspensive appeal (stays execution) within 30 days, with the security posted within the same 30 days, both counted from the end of the 7-day new-trial/JNOV delay (exclusive of legal holidays) or from mailing of notice denying such a motion. Miss the 30 days and the judgment can be executed. Appellees wanting relief must answer the appeal within 15 days after the return day or lodging.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1974, 2087, 2121, 2123, 2124, 2133, 2252"},{"t":"Partial rulings are interlocutory: use a writ, fast","d":"A judgment, partial summary judgment, or sustained exception disposing of fewer than all claims, issues, or theories by or against a party is interlocutory under art. 1915(C) as amended by Acts 2025, No. 250, with no designation procedure in the current text. Review is by supervisory writ: give notice of intent and get a return date, which may not exceed 30 days from notice of the ruling. Denials of exceptions and of summary judgment are likewise reviewed only by writ (and a summary-judgment denial is never appealable).","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 968, 1914, 1915(C), 2083(C); Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-2, 4-3"},{"t":"Never plead a dollar amount; know the jury threshold and bond","d":"A petition may not state a specific amount of damages (except in suits on contracts, notes, open accounts, and similar claims); use a general allegation that the claim exceeds or is less than a threshold when it matters, such as for federal diversity or the jury right. A specific amount is struck on motion and can bring fees. No jury where no plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000; a jury demand is due within 10 days after service of the last pleading on a jury issue and requires a bond (or a $5,000 cash deposit within 60 days in tort cases stipulated between $10,000 and $50,000).","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 893, 1732, 1733, 1734"},{"t":"Summary judgment has hard, fixed deadlines","d":"Unless the court extends and all parties agree: the motion and supporting documents must be filed and served at least 65 days before trial, the opposition at least 15 days before the hearing, and the reply at least 5 days before (counting legal holidays). The hearing must be set at least 30 days after filing and 30 days before trial, and judgment rendered at least 20 days before trial. Only listed kinds of documents count, and evidentiary objections go in the opposition or reply. Documents filed late are not considered.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 966(A)(4), (B), (C), (D)(2)"},{"t":"Answer in 21 days; defaults move fast and need only 7 days’ notice in some cases","d":"The answer is due 21 days after service of citation (30 if discovery was served with the petition). There is no preliminary default: once the delay runs, the plaintiff may obtain a default judgment on a prima facie case. Notice of intent to take a default is required, at least 7 days before, only if the defendant appeared of record, the defendant’s lawyer wrote to the plaintiff about the case, or it is a tort case. A defendant may still answer any time before the default is signed. New-trial delays then run 7 days from notice.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1001, 1002, 1702(A), 1974"},{"t":"District court filing: paper in person or e-file only since January 1, 2026","d":"Since January 1, 2026, attorneys’ filings in district court may be made only in person on paper or electronically through the clerk’s system or the Louisiana Clerks’ Remote Access Authority (with listed original documents still allowed by mail or courier), and statutory fax filing ended January 1, 2026. Filings must omit the first five digits of SSNs, tax and account numbers, and other protected information. Do not plan to fax a motion for appeal or new trial on the last day.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 253(B)(2)–(3); La. R.S. 13:850(A)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice starts at the Disciplinary Board and closes 30 days before trial","d":"Before moving the court, the out-of-state lawyer must file a verified application with the Louisiana Attorney Disciplinary Board and pay $450, then obtain Disciplinary Counsel’s letter, which Louisiana counsel attaches to an ex parte motion served on all parties. Any party or Disciplinary Counsel may object within 20 days. Absent extraordinary circumstances explained in the motion, courts will not consider a motion filed within 30 days of trial or hearing. Louisiana counsel must remain associated and responsible throughout.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XVII, § 13(A)(1)–(4); La. R.S. 37:214"},{"t":"Local motion practice: 15-day memoranda and judgment circulation","d":"Under the statewide District Court Rules, the mover’s memorandum must be received by all parties 15 calendar days before the hearing and the opposition 8 calendar days before; replies must arrive by 4:00 p.m. leaving one full working day before the hearing. Proposed judgments not signed at the hearing must be circulated at least 5 working days before presentation, with a Rule 9.5(b) certificate. Late memoranda can cost oral argument and the other side’s costs.","c":"La. Dist. Ct. R. 9.5(b), 9.8(c), 9.9(b)–(e)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal for cause only","d":"The Code of Civil Procedure’s recusal chapter (arts. 151–161) has no peremptory challenge or automatic change of judge. A judge must be recused if the judge is a witness; was employed or consulted as a lawyer in the cause and participated; is the spouse of a party or lawyer, or has a parent, child, or immediate family member who is a party or lawyer; or is biased, prejudiced, or interested to the point of being unable to be fair. A catch-all also requires recusal when a substantial and objective basis would reasonably be expected to prevent fair and impartial handling of any aspect of the cause. Being a taxpayer or resident of an interested public body, or a member of an interested religious body, is not alone a ground.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 151","v":true},{"t":"Recusal motion: 30 days from discovery and before the case is set for trial","d":"File a written motion stating a ground under art. 151 no later than 30 days after discovering the facts, and in all cases before the matter is scheduled for trial (or immediately, if the facts arise or could not have been discovered until after scheduling). Within 7 days after receiving the motion, the judge must either step aside or ask the Supreme Court to appoint an ad hoc judge to hear it; only that ad hoc judge may act in the case while the motion is pending. An untimely motion, or one stating no art. 151 ground, may be denied by the challenged judge without a hearing, with written reasons.\nIf recusal is granted in a multi-judge court, the case is randomly reassigned to another division.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 154, 155, 156(A)","v":true},{"t":"Judges must disclose certain relationships","d":"Trial and appellate judges must disclose to all lawyers and self-represented parties: a past association with a lawyer during that lawyer’s employment in the cause; continuing to employ the handling lawyer (or that lawyer’s firm) personally at the time of a contested hearing; having performed a judicial act in the cause in another court; kinship with a party or party’s spouse within the fourth degree, or with a lawyer in the cause, the lawyer’s spouse, or a member of the lawyer’s firm within the second degree; and a substantial economic interest of the judge’s spouse, parent, child, or immediate family member in the subject matter. After disclosure, any party may move to recuse under art. 151.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 152","v":true},{"t":"Appellate judges and Supreme Court justices","d":"Recusal of court of appeal judges and Supreme Court justices has its own procedure (C.C.P. arts. 157–161; Supreme Court Rule XXXVI for justices). Those texts were not collected.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 157–161 (not collected); La. Sup. Ct. R. XXXVI (not collected)","v":false}],"speech":[{"t":"La. Const. art. I, § 7 | Breadth relative to the First Amendment not established here","d":"Article I, § 7 of the Louisiana Constitution protects freedom of expression. Neither the constitutional text nor case law comparing it with the First Amendment was collected, so no claim is made that Louisiana protects speech more broadly. The practical speech protection documented here is procedural: the art. 971 special motion to strike.","c":"La. Const. art. I, § 7 (not collected); La. C.C.P. art. 971","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Civil-law vocabulary: petition, exceptions, reconvention, prescription","d":"Louisiana procedure uses its own terms. A complaint is a petition; Rule 12 defenses are exceptions (declinatory, dilatory, peremptory); a counterclaim is a reconventional demand; joinder of claims is cumulation; a statute of limitations is prescription (and a non-interruptible period is peremption); a notice of appeal is an order of appeal; a cross-appeal is an answer to the appeal. Replicatory pleadings (replies) are prohibited, and new matter in an answer is treated as denied.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 852, 891, 921–927, 1031, 1061, 2121, 2133","v":true},{"t":"Transactional res judicata","d":"A party must assert all causes of action arising out of the transaction or occurrence that is the subject of the litigation, with family-law exceptions. Claims left out can be lost.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 425","v":true},{"t":"Non-unanimous civil juries and a $10,000 jury threshold","d":"Civil verdicts need 5 of 6 or 9 of 12 jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise. No jury is available where no individual plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000, and a party demanding a jury must post a bond or, in mid-sized tort cases, a $5,000 cash deposit.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1732, 1733, 1797","v":true},{"t":"Random allotment and statewide district court rules","d":"Every case is randomly allotted to a division (C.C.P. art. 253.1). The Rules for Louisiana District Courts apply statewide, with district-by-district appendices: memoranda on exceptions and motions must be received 15 calendar days before the hearing (oppositions 8 days; replies by 4:00 p.m. with one full working day to spare), no hearing is set less than 15 days after filing without good cause, proposed judgments must be circulated 5 working days before presentation with a Rule 9.5(b) certificate, and a Rule 10.1 conference and certificate are required before a motion to compel.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 253.1; La. Dist. Ct. R. 9.5, 9.8, 9.9, 10.1","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Louisiana Code of Civil Procedure, Book III (arts. 2081–2201) and related articles (1911–1918, 1974, 1811, 3612, 5001–5002, 5059), official text from legis.la.gov, current through Acts 2026 (e.g., Acts 2026, No. 127). Uniform Rules of Louisiana Courts of Appeal, with amendments effective July 1, 2026 (lasc.org PDF). Rules of the Supreme Court of Louisiana, Rules IX (Rehearings), X (Writ Applications), and XLII (Electronic Filing), as rendered on lasc.org on November 10, 2026. Supreme Court rule-change orders list (lasc.org). Local rules of the five circuits were not collected.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Louisiana: get an order of appeal, choose devolutive or suspensive, and know when you need a writ instead","d":"A Louisiana appeal is not started by filing a notice of appeal. You move the trial court for an order of appeal, which sets the return day; the clerk then mails the notice of appeal. The delay depends on the kind of appeal: a devolutive appeal (no stay of execution, no bond) has 60 days, while a suspensive appeal (stays execution) has only 30 days and the security must be posted within those 30 days. Both clocks start when the new-trial/JNOV delay expires (7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after notice of judgment) or when notice of denial of a timely motion is mailed.\nOnly final judgments, partial final judgments listed in art. 1915(A), and interlocutory judgments made appealable by law can be appealed. Everything else (most interlocutory rulings, including, since Acts 2025, No. 250, a partial summary judgment or exception that disposes of fewer than all claims against a party) is reviewed by supervisory writ, which runs on a return date of no more than 30 days from notice of the ruling.\nThere is no cross-appeal notice: an appellee who wants the judgment changed must answer the appeal within 15 days after the return day or lodging. Further review in the Supreme Court is almost entirely by discretionary writ of certiorari, due 30 days after transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1811, 1974, 2083, 2087, 2121, 2123, 2133, 2166; Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-3; La. Sup. Ct. R. X"},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final judgments and listed partial final judgments","d":"A final judgment is appealable whether after trial, by default, or reformed by remittitur or additur. A partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) is appealable: dismissal of fewer than all parties; judgment on the pleadings; summary judgment (other than one deciding only a particular issue under art. 966(E)); judgment on a separately tried principal or incidental demand; a separately tried liability judgment; and certain sanctions orders. Other partial final judgments are appealable only if a law expressly says so.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1841, 1915(A), 2083(A)–(B)"},{"t":"Partial rulings on fewer than all claims are interlocutory","d":"When a court grants a judgment or summary judgment, or sustains an exception, as to one or more but fewer than all of the claims, demands, issues, or theories by or against a party, that judgment is interlocutory. The current text of art. 1915 (as amended by Acts 2025, No. 250) contains no mechanism to designate such a ruling final for immediate appeal. Seek a supervisory writ instead. Lawyers used to the old “no just reason for delay” designation should not rely on it.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1915(C), 2083(C)"},{"t":"Interlocutory judgments only when a law allows","d":"An interlocutory judgment is appealable only when expressly provided by law. Examples in the collected text: orders relating to a preliminary or final injunction (appeal within 15 days; no appeal from TRO orders), and the order of seizure and sale in executory proceedings (suspensive appeal within 15 days of service of the notice of seizure). A judgment on class certification is appealable as of right, suspensively or devolutively (art. 592).","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2083(C), 3612, 2642, 592"},{"t":"Signed judgment with decretal language required","d":"No appeal lies until the final judgment is signed. A final judgment must be identified as final, signed and dated, and must name the party who wins, the party who loses, and the relief. If an appealed judgment lacks proper decretal language, the appellate court remands for the trial court to amend it under art. 1951. Reasons for judgment go in a separate opinion; non-jury reasons must be given if requested within 10 days after mailing of notice of judgment.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1911(B), 1917(A), 1918"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Order of appeal, not a notice","d":"Obtain an order of appeal from the trial court within the delay, on oral motion in open court, written motion, or petition. The order shows the return day and the security when the court must set it. The clerk mails notice of the appeal to the other parties and the appellate court; the clerk’s failure does not invalidate the appeal. Within 7 days of the order of appeal, the clerk of the trial court transmits the notice of appeal to the court of appeal and the Supreme Court’s judicial administrator.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2121; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-2.1"},{"t":"Devolutive: 60 days; suspensive: 30 days and bond","d":"Devolutive appeal: within 60 days of (1) expiry of the new-trial/JNOV delay if no motion was timely filed, or (2) mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion. Suspensive appeal: within 30 days of the same events, and the security must be furnished within those 30 days. If the suspensive bond is not timely filed, the trial court may convert the appeal to devolutive (not in eviction cases).","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2087(A), 2123(A), 2088(B)"},{"t":"Tolling: new trial and JNOV","d":"A motion for new trial or for JNOV is due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers in open court, or the sheriff serves, notice of judgment. If any party files one, the appeal delays for all parties start when they start for the party whose motion is decided last. Notice of denial must be mailed, and the appeal delay runs from that mailing. An order of appeal granted while such a motion is pending is premature but becomes effective when the motions are denied. The court must decide a new-trial motion within 10 days after submission unless all counsel stipulate otherwise.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 1811(A), 1914(C), 1974, 1979, 2087(C)–(D), 2123(B)–(C)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals: answer the appeal or take your own","d":"An appellee who wants the judgment modified, revised, or reversed, or who wants damages for a frivolous appeal, must file an answer to the appeal stating the relief within 15 days after the return day or the lodging of the record, whichever is later. Alternatively, an appellee may take its own devolutive appeal within the normal 60 days or within 10 days after the clerk mails notice of the first devolutive appeal, whichever is later. An appellee who only defends the judgment may argue any ground supported by the record without appealing.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2087(B), 2133"},{"t":"Removal to federal court interrupts the delays","d":"Filing a notice of removal under 28 U.S.C. § 1446 interrupts the devolutive and suspensive appeal delays for all parties; they start anew on remand.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2087(E), 2123(D)"}]],["Security, costs, and the record",[{"t":"Suspensive appeal security","d":"No security for a devolutive appeal. For a money judgment, suspensive-appeal security equals the judgment plus interest to the date furnished, excluding costs; for good cause the court may require up to 150% with a surety bond; for judgments over $150 million (outside listed exceptions) the court may set a lower amount after a hearing. A party aggrieved by the amount may seek a priority supervisory writ, which interrupts the suspensive-appeal delay. Other judgments: security fixed to assure satisfaction plus damages for delay. A creditor may not execute until the suspensive-appeal delay has run.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2124, 2252"},{"t":"Pay estimated costs within 20 days","d":"After the order of appeal, the clerk estimates the cost of the record (including transcript and appellate filing fee) and sends notice. The appellant must pay within 20 days after transmission of the notice (or of a revised notice after designation). The trial court may grant one 20-day extension for good cause. If the appellant does not pay in time, the trial judge, after a hearing, must either dismiss the appeal as abandoned or give 10 more days to pay, failing which it is dismissed. Once costs are paid, a missed return day or late lodging does not cause dismissal.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2126, 2088(A)(9)"},{"t":"Return day and lodging","d":"Return day: 30 days after estimated costs are paid, or 45 days if testimony must be transcribed, unless the judge sets less. The trial court may grant one extension of up to 30 days; later extensions come from the court of appeal. The trial clerk lodges the certified record on or before the return day, and the court of appeal clerk notifies counsel of the lodging date.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2125, 2127; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-2.2, 2-2.3"},{"t":"Designating a partial record","d":"The appellant may designate portions of the record within 10 days after the cost notice; others may counter-designate within 14 days after service. If you designate a partial record, you must serve a concise statement of the points you rely on, and the appeal is limited to them. Otherwise no assignment of errors is required in the trial court.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2128, 2129"}]],["Briefing in the court of appeal",[{"t":"Brief deadlines run from lodging","d":"Appellant’s brief: 25 calendar days after the record is lodged. Appellee’s brief: 45 calendar days after lodging. Reply: 10 calendar days after the appellee’s brief. When several parties obtain orders of appeal, the earlier order’s party briefs on the appellant schedule and the later on the appellee schedule unless the court directs otherwise.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7"},{"t":"Extensions and abandonment","d":"Extensions require a written motion filed on or before the due date, for good cause; an appellant’s extension automatically extends the appellee for 20 days from the extended date; no extension that would delay the hearing. A filed motion to suspend briefing does not itself suspend anything. If the appellant misses the brief deadline, the clerk sends a notice; if no brief is filed within 30 days after that notice, the appeal is subject to dismissal as abandoned. A late brief forfeits oral argument.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-8.6, 2-8.7, 2-12.8, 2-12.12"},{"t":"Page limits and format","d":"Original appellant and appellee briefs: 31 pages on legal-size paper or 41 pages on letter-size; reply briefs 13 (legal) or 18 (letter). Excluded: cover, tables, the judgment and reasons, and certificate of service. Times New Roman 14-point or larger, double-spaced, 1-inch margins. Leave to exceed must be sought before the due date. The appellant’s brief must include a jurisdictional statement and attach the judgment and the reasons. Briefs must be courteous; offensive language can bring contempt and return of the brief.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.2, 2-12.4"},{"t":"Filing and service","d":"File with the clerk by delivery, mail, or (if local rule permits) fax or e-filing. Mailing on or before the due date is timely, shown by a USPS postmark or receipt or a bona fide commercial courier record (not a private meter stamp). E-filed or faxed documents are timely if received by the due date. Serve every filing on opposing counsel and unrepresented parties under C.C.P. art. 1313, with a certificate listing all parties and counsel.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-13, 2-14"},{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Up to 40 minutes total, divided equally between sides, unless the court allows more; the appellant opens and closes. Do not read argument from a prepared text. Amicus curiae may not request argument.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-15, 2-12.11"},{"t":"Motions to dismiss for appellant’s irregularities: 3 days","d":"A motion to dismiss an appeal for an irregularity, error, or defect imputable to the appellant must be filed within 3 days, exclusive of holidays, after the return day or lodging, whichever is later. Dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, no right to appeal, consent, or abandonment may be sought any time. Defects in the record are not grounds for dismissal.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2161, 2162"}]],["Supervisory writs (interlocutory review)",[{"t":"Notice of intent and a return date of 30 days or less","d":"Give notice of intent to apply for writs to the opposing parties and, at the same time, ask the trial judge to set a return date. In civil cases the return date may not exceed 30 days from the date of notice of the judgment under art. 1914. The judge must set an explicit return date. Extensions require a motion filed within the original or extended period. A late application is not considered unless the delay was not the applicant’s fault, and the application must document the return date and any extensions.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-2, 4-3; La. C.C.P. art. 1914"},{"t":"Contents and stays","d":"The application must be verified by affidavit certifying service on the respondent judge and all parties, with addresses, phone numbers, and emails. A writ application does not stay trial-court proceedings unless the trial court or court of appeal orders it; ask the trial court first. Requests for expedited consideration need a bold cover statement, a separate “REQUEST FOR EXPEDITED CONSIDERATION” page, and an affidavit of prompt notice and equal-means service.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-4, 4-5"},{"t":"Expedited cases involving minors","d":"Child in need of care, delinquency, termination, adoption, custody modifications, and similar cases get preferential handling and initials are used for minors.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 5-1, 5-2"}]],["Rehearing",[{"t":"Court of appeal rehearing: 14 days, no extensions","d":"In civil cases, apply within 14 days after transmission of notice of the judgment; no extension is allowed. Maximum 10 pages without permission; the supporting brief is filed with the application (more time may be requested in the same filing). Rehearing is considered only when the court granted a writ on the merits, dismissed an appeal, or ruled on the merits of an appeal; no oral argument.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A); Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-18"},{"t":"Supreme Court rehearing: 14 days","d":"Apply within 14 days after transmission of notice of judgment. Available only when the Court granted a writ, an appeal, or another proceeding; reconsideration of a writ denial is not considered absent extraordinary circumstances. Application and any opposition: 25 pages each; appendix up to 25 pages in civil cases. No rehearing in election cases.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2167(A); La. Sup. Ct. R. IX"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court of Louisiana",[{"t":"Certiorari: 30 days, discretionary","d":"Apply for a writ of certiorari within 30 days after transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or within 30 days after transmission of notice denying a timely rehearing application. If one party timely applies, another may apply within the original 30 days or within 10 days after the Supreme Court clerk transmits notice of the first application, whichever is later. “Transmission” means sending by U.S. mail or email. Grant is discretionary; Rule X lists the considerations (conflicts, significant unresolved issues, precedent to be overruled, erroneous application of law causing material injustice or affecting the public interest, gross departure from proper proceedings).","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A)–(D), (F); La. Sup. Ct. R. X, § 1"},{"t":"Civil writ application requirements","d":"Include the filing sheet, an index, a statement of which Rule X consideration applies, a memorandum of no more than 25 pages (statement of the case, assignments of error, summary of argument, argument), a verification, and the dated trial-court ruling with reasons and the court of appeal’s order and opinion (including rehearing rulings). Appendix of other documents: 25 pages, separately bound, no court of appeal briefs. Filing fee under La. R.S. 13:126.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. X, §§ 2–3"},{"t":"Oppositions and replies","d":"Opposition: within 30 days of the Court’s acknowledgment letter, no extension except on clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control (calendaring errors do not count); 25 pages. Reply: within 10 days of the opposition, 7 pages including exhibits, discouraged. If the writ is granted, the record must be lodged within 14 days and the case is briefed under Rule IV.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. X, §§ 5–7"},{"t":"Finality","d":"A court of appeal judgment becomes final if no timely rehearing or certiorari application is filed; if certiorari is timely sought and denied, it becomes final 5 days (exclusive of legal holidays) after the clerk mails the denial. The Supreme Court may stay execution pending certiorari or U.S. Supreme Court review.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A), (E)"}]],["E-filing and filing mechanics",[{"t":"Supreme Court: CDX","d":"Louisiana-admitted lawyers may e-file in the Supreme Court through the Court Data/Document Exchange after mandatory training; filings must be text-searchable PDF/A and obey all page limits. Timely if completed before midnight Central Time on the due date (processing after 4:30 p.m. occurs the next business day). Rejected filings may be re-filed within 7 calendar days and keep the original time. Call the clerk before e-filing a priority or stay request.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. XLII, §§ 2–4, 6, 7, 9"},{"t":"Courts of appeal and trial courts","d":"Courts of appeal accept e-filing and fax only if their local rules permit (local rules not collected). In district courts, since January 1, 2026 attorneys may file only in person on paper or electronically through the clerk’s system or the Louisiana Clerks’ Remote Access Authority, and fax filing under R.S. 13:850 ended January 1, 2026; this matters for filing a motion for appeal or new trial at the last minute.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-13; La. C.C.P. art. 253(B)(2); La. R.S. 13:850(A)"}]],["Other courts",[{"t":"City and parish court appeals: 10 days","d":"Appeals from city and parish courts go to the court of appeal on the record but must be taken within 10 days of the judgment or of service of notice of judgment when notice is required. Appeals from justice of the peace courts are by suit for trial de novo in the parish or district court within 15 days.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 5001, 5002, 4924, 4925"},{"t":"Eviction appeals: 24 hours to suspend","d":"An appeal does not suspend an eviction judgment unless the defendant answered under oath with an affirmative defense and applied for appeal and filed the bond within 24 hours after the judgment.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 4735"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals and unpublished opinions","d":"The appellate court may award damages, including attorney fees, for a frivolous appeal or writ application and may tax costs as it finds equitable. Unpublished Supreme Court and court of appeal opinions are posted online and may be cited by case name and the court’s number.","c":"La. C.C.P. arts. 2164, 2168; Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-19"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Motion for new trial","d":"7 days from clerk’s mailing or delivery in open court, or sheriff’s service, of notice of judgment (art. 1913).\nExclusive of legal holidays. Tolls appeal delays for all parties.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 1974","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s mailing or delivery in open court, or sheriff’s service, of notice of judgment)","note":"Exclusive of legal holidays. Tolls appeal delays for all parties."}},{"t":"Motion for JNOV","d":"7 days from notice of judgment (or jury discharge if no verdict).\nExclusive of legal holidays.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 1811(A)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (notice of judgment)","note":"Exclusive of legal holidays."}},{"t":"Devolutive appeal (order of appeal)","d":"60 days from expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion.\nNo bond. Does not stay execution.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2087(A)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion)","note":"No bond. Does not stay execution."}},{"t":"Suspensive appeal (order of appeal and security)","d":"30 days from expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion.\nSecurity must also be furnished within the 30 days; late bond allows conversion to devolutive.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2123(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion)","note":"Security must also be furnished within the 30 days; late bond allows conversion to devolutive."}},{"t":"Appellee’s own devolutive appeal","d":"10 days from clerk’s mailing of notice of the first devolutive appeal.\nOr within the normal 60 days, whichever is later.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2087(B)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s mailing of notice of the first devolutive appeal)","note":"Or within the normal 60 days, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Answer to appeal (cross-relief)","d":"15 days from return day or lodging of the record, whichever is later.\nRequired to obtain any change in the judgment.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2133(A)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (return day or lodging of the record, whichever is later)","note":"Required to obtain any change in the judgment."}},{"t":"Pay estimated appeal costs","d":"20 days from transmission of the clerk’s notice of estimated costs.\nOne extension of up to 20 days for good cause.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2126","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of the clerk’s notice of estimated costs)","note":"One extension of up to 20 days for good cause."}},{"t":"Designate partial record","d":"10 days from transmission of notice of estimated costs.\nOther parties may counter-designate within 14 days after service.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2128","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of notice of estimated costs)","note":"Other parties may counter-designate within 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Return day","d":"30 days from payment of estimated costs.\n45 days if testimony must be transcribed; one trial-court extension of up to 30 days.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2125","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (payment of estimated costs)","note":"45 days if testimony must be transcribed; one trial-court extension of up to 30 days."}},{"t":"Motion to dismiss appeal for appellant’s irregularity","d":"3 days from return day or lodging, whichever is later.\nExclusive of holidays.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2161","due":{"n":3,"from":"the trigger date (return day or lodging, whichever is later)","note":"Exclusive of holidays."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"25 days from lodging of the record.\nCalendar days.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","due":{"n":25,"from":"the trigger date (lodging of the record)","note":"Calendar days."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"45 days from lodging of the record.\nExtended 20 days beyond any extension granted to the appellant.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (lodging of the record)","note":"Extended 20 days beyond any extension granted to the appellant."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"10 days from filing of appellee’s brief.\nConfined to rebuttal.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellee’s brief)","note":"Confined to rebuttal."}},{"t":"Brief after abandonment notice","d":"30 days from clerk’s transmission of the abandonment notice.\nOtherwise the appeal is subject to dismissal as abandoned.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-8.6","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s transmission of the abandonment notice)","note":"Otherwise the appeal is subject to dismissal as abandoned."}},{"t":"Supervisory writ application (civil)","d":"30 days from notice of the interlocutory judgment under art. 1914.\nMaximum; the actual deadline is the return date the judge sets, which may be shorter.","c":"Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-3","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notice of the interlocutory judgment under art. 1914)","note":"Maximum; the actual deadline is the return date the judge sets, which may be shorter."}},{"t":"Preliminary-injunction appeal","d":"15 days from date of the order or judgment.\nBond, if required, also within 15 days. No appeal from TRO orders.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 3612(C)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (date of the order or judgment)","note":"Bond, if required, also within 15 days. No appeal from TRO orders."}},{"t":"Court of appeal rehearing","d":"14 days from transmission of notice of judgment.\nNo extensions.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A); Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-18.2","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of notice of judgment)","note":"No extensions."}},{"t":"Supreme Court certiorari","d":"30 days from transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or of notice of denial of rehearing.\nSecond applicant: later of 30 days or 10 days after notice of the first application.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A)–(C)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or of notice of denial of rehearing)","note":"Second applicant: later of 30 days or 10 days after notice of the first application."}},{"t":"Opposition to Supreme Court writ application","d":"30 days from date of the Court’s acknowledgment letter.\nNo extensions absent clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control.","c":"La. Sup. Ct. R. X, § 5","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Court’s acknowledgment letter)","note":"No extensions absent clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control."}},{"t":"Supreme Court rehearing","d":"14 days from transmission of notice of judgment.\nOnly after a grant of writ, appeal, or other proceeding.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 2167(A)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of notice of judgment)","note":"Only after a grant of writ, appeal, or other proceeding."}},{"t":"City or parish court appeal","d":"10 days from judgment, or service of notice of judgment when required.\nTo the court of appeal.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 5002","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (judgment, or service of notice of judgment when required)","note":"To the court of appeal."}},{"t":"Suspensive appeal from eviction","d":"1 days from rendition of judgment of eviction.\n24 hours; also requires a sworn answer pleading an affirmative defense.","c":"La. C.C.P. art. 4735","due":{"n":1,"from":"the trigger date (rendition of judgment of eviction)","note":"24 hours; also requires a sworn answer pleading an affirmative defense."}}]]],"dl":[["Motion for new trial",7,"Clerk’s mailing or delivery in open court, or sheriff’s service, of notice of judgment (art. 1913)","La. C.C.P. art. 1974","Exclusive of legal holidays. Tolls appeal delays for all parties."],["Motion for JNOV",7,"Notice of judgment (or jury discharge if no verdict)","La. C.C.P. art. 1811(A)","Exclusive of legal holidays."],["Devolutive appeal (order of appeal)",60,"Expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion","La. C.C.P. art. 2087(A)","No bond. Does not stay execution."],["Suspensive appeal (order of appeal and security)",30,"Expiry of new-trial/JNOV delay, or mailing of notice of denial of a timely motion","La. C.C.P. art. 2123(A)","Security must also be furnished within the 30 days; late bond allows conversion to devolutive."],["Appellee’s own devolutive appeal",10,"Clerk’s mailing of notice of the first devolutive appeal","La. C.C.P. art. 2087(B)","Or within the normal 60 days, whichever is later."],["Answer to appeal (cross-relief)",15,"Return day or lodging of the record, whichever is later","La. C.C.P. art. 2133(A)","Required to obtain any change in the judgment."],["Pay estimated appeal costs",20,"Transmission of the clerk’s notice of estimated costs","La. C.C.P. art. 2126","One extension of up to 20 days for good cause."],["Designate partial record",10,"Transmission of notice of estimated costs","La. C.C.P. art. 2128","Other parties may counter-designate within 14 days after service."],["Return day",30,"Payment of estimated costs","La. C.C.P. art. 2125","45 days if testimony must be transcribed; one trial-court extension of up to 30 days."],["Motion to dismiss appeal for appellant’s irregularity",3,"Return day or lodging, whichever is later","La. C.C.P. art. 2161","Exclusive of holidays."],["Appellant’s brief",25,"Lodging of the record","Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","Calendar days."],["Appellee’s brief",45,"Lodging of the record","Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","Extended 20 days beyond any extension granted to the appellant."],["Reply brief",10,"Filing of appellee’s brief","Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-12.7","Confined to rebuttal."],["Brief after abandonment notice",30,"Clerk’s transmission of the abandonment notice","Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-8.6","Otherwise the appeal is subject to dismissal as abandoned."],["Supervisory writ application (civil)",30,"Notice of the interlocutory judgment under art. 1914","Unif. R. Ct. App. 4-3","Maximum; the actual deadline is the return date the judge sets, which may be shorter."],["Preliminary-injunction appeal",15,"Date of the order or judgment","La. C.C.P. art. 3612(C)","Bond, if required, also within 15 days. No appeal from TRO orders."],["Court of appeal rehearing",14,"Transmission of notice of judgment","La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A); Unif. R. Ct. App. 2-18.2","No extensions."],["Supreme Court certiorari",30,"Transmission of the court of appeal’s notice of judgment, or of notice of denial of rehearing","La. C.C.P. art. 2166(A)–(C)","Second applicant: later of 30 days or 10 days after notice of the first application."],["Opposition to Supreme Court writ application",30,"Date of the Court’s acknowledgment letter","La. Sup. Ct. R. X, § 5","No extensions absent clear and convincing proof of circumstances beyond the party’s control."],["Supreme Court rehearing",14,"Transmission of notice of judgment","La. C.C.P. art. 2167(A)","Only after a grant of writ, appeal, or other proceeding."],["City or parish court appeal",10,"Judgment, or service of notice of judgment when required","La. C.C.P. art. 5002","To the court of appeal."],["Suspensive appeal from eviction",1,"Rendition of judgment of eviction","La. C.C.P. art. 4735","24 hours; also requires a sworn answer pleading an affirmative defense."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.lasc.org/"]]},"rulesName":"Louisiana Code of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"NOTE","t":"What this file covers (a selection)","s":"Louisiana civil procedure is the Code of Civil Procedure (La. C.C.P.), a statute of roughly 1,260 articles in nine Books, not court rules. This file is a SELECTION: it summarizes about 300 key articles, grouped by Book and Title, read from the official text on legis.la.gov on November 10, 2026 (the site reflects acts through the 2026 Regular Session, e.g., Acts 2026, No. 127 amending art. 561). Articles not listed here (most of Books III–IX on executory process, successions, tutorship, and other special proceedings) were not summarized. Group rows (for example “921–934”) cover a run of related articles. Comparisons to the Federal Rules are Consi’s and are noted only where the difference is plain from the text. A few points rest only on an article’s official heading (from the legis.la.gov table of contents) and are marked as such or cite the heading; full article texts collected are in text/ccp_selected.txt.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK I","t":"Courts, Actions, and Parties (arts. 1–821)","s":"Jurisdiction, venue, recusal, court officers, actions (cumulation, lis pendens, abandonment, class actions), and parties.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1–5","t":"Subject-matter jurisdiction","s":"Jurisdiction over the subject matter cannot be conferred by consent; a judgment by a court without it is void (art. 3). When jurisdiction depends on amount, the amount demanded in good faith controls (art. 4). A plaintiff who reduces a claim to fit a court’s jurisdiction remits the excess and cannot sue for it later (art. 5).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"6","t":"Personal jurisdiction","s":"Personal jurisdiction rests on service on the defendant or its agent (or an express waiver under art. 1201), service on a court-appointed attorney for an absent or incompetent Louisiana domiciliary, or submission (by suing, or by not timely filing the declinatory exception). Over nonresidents, courts may exercise jurisdiction on any basis consistent with the state and federal constitutions.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"41–45","t":"Venue: general rules","s":"Venue is the parish where an action may properly be brought. Individuals are sued in the parish of domicile (or residence); domestic corporations, insurers, and LLCs where the registered office is; licensed foreign corporations and LLCs at the principal business establishment designated with the state; unlicensed foreign entities and nonresidents without an agent in the plaintiff’s parish or where served (art. 42). Venue objections cannot be waived before suit and some venues (art. 44(B)) can never be waived; otherwise an objection is waived if the declinatory exception is not timely pleaded under art. 928 (art. 44(C)). Art. 45 resolves conflicts among venue articles.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"71–86","t":"Venue: exceptions","s":"Selected: solidary obligors may be sued where venue is proper under art. 42 as to any of them, with a special rule for tort claims (art. 73); tort suits may be filed where the wrongful conduct occurred or where damages were sustained (art. 74); contract suits where the contract was executed or the work was or was to be performed (art. 76.1). Other articles fix venue for immovable property, successions, partition, and similar actions.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"121–124","t":"Improper venue; change of venue; forum non conveniens","s":"An action in an improper venue may be dismissed or, in the interest of justice, transferred (art. 121). A party may obtain a change of venue to a parish where no party is domiciled on proof that a fair trial is impossible (art. 122). Art. 123 allows forum non conveniens transfer between district courts on contradictory motion, but not of a suit filed in the plaintiff’s home parish in an otherwise proper court; dismissal in favor of a foreign forum is conditioned on the defendant waiving prescription if suit is filed there within 60 days.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"151–161","t":"Recusal of judges","s":"Mandatory grounds for recusal of any trial or appellate judge, plus a catch-all where a substantial and objective basis would prevent fair handling (art. 151). Judges must disclose listed relationships (art. 152). A motion to recuse a district judge must be filed within 30 days of discovering the facts and before the case is set for trial; within 7 days the judge must recuse or ask the Supreme Court for an ad hoc judge, and an untimely or groundless motion may be denied with written reasons and no hearing (art. 154). There is no peremptory challenge of a judge.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"191–197","t":"Power and authority of courts","s":"Courts have inherent power needed to exercise their jurisdiction (art. 191). Courts may adopt local rules not contrary to law (art. 193). In a civil proceeding that needs no witness testimony or evidence, a party may give written notice at least 10 days before the hearing that it will appear remotely, and a court with the technology must allow it unless an exception in art. 195.1 applies.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"221–227","t":"Contempt","s":"Contempt is direct (in the court’s presence, or contumacious failure to obey a subpoena or summons) or constructive (for example, willful disobedience of a judgment or order). No one may be held in contempt except for conduct defined as such by law; punishment is in R.S. 13:4611.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"251–259","t":"Clerks of court","s":"All pleadings, documents, and exhibits are delivered or transmitted to the clerk, who endorses the filing date (art. 253). All pleadings are randomly allotted to a section or division of court (art. 253.1). (Clerk e-filing statutes, such as R.S. 13:850, were not collected.)","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"371","t":"Attorney as officer of the court","s":"An attorney must act with decorum and treat the court, its officers, jurors, witnesses, opposing parties, and opposing counsel with due respect; the article lists further duties.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"421–428","t":"Actions: general","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a pleading presenting the demand to a court of competent jurisdiction; amicable demand is not required unless a law says so (art. 421). An action is premature until the obligation is enforceable (art. 423). Res judicata in Louisiana is transactional: a party must assert all causes of action arising out of the transaction or occurrence that is the subject of the litigation, with family-law exceptions (art. 425).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"461–465","t":"Cumulation of actions (joinder of claims and parties)","s":"A plaintiff may cumulate actions against one defendant if each is within the court’s jurisdiction, in proper venue, mutually consistent, and uses the same form of procedure (art. 462). Joinder of plural parties also requires a community of interest (art. 463). Improper cumulation is raised by dilatory exception (art. 926); if jurisdiction or venue fails as to one action, that action is dismissed (art. 464).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"531–532","t":"Lis pendens","s":"When two suits on the same transaction or occurrence between the same parties in the same capacities are pending in Louisiana courts, the defendant may have all but the first dismissed by declinatory exception (art. 531). If the first suit is in another state or federal court, the Louisiana court may stay the second suit instead (art. 532).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"561","t":"Abandonment (3 years without a step)","s":"An action is abandoned, without any court order, when the parties take no step in its prosecution or defense in the trial court for three years; formal discovery served on a party, filed or not, is a step. After the period runs the defendant may renounce abandonment. As amended in 2026, a motion to dismiss must attach an affidavit listing record filings during the prior three years and written communications in the final year; the court signs an ex parte dismissal without prejudice only if none exist, and otherwise may sign it or set a hearing. The sheriff serves the dismissal; a motion to set it aside is due within 30 days of service, and an appeal within 60 days. Dismissal of a suit on a conventional obligation still interrupts prescription. No federal analog: unlike Rule 41(b), this is automatic.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"591–597","t":"Class actions","s":"Prerequisites track FRCP 23(a) (numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy) plus a fifth requirement that the class be objectively definable without inquiring into each member’s merits, and (b) categories modeled on FRCP 23(b) (art. 591). The proponent must move to certify within 90 days after service of the initial class pleading on all adverse parties, extendable by stipulation or for good cause (art. 592). Filing a class petition suspends prescription for class members until 30 days after an opt-out, exclusion notice, or dismissal notice (art. 596).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"611–617","t":"Derivative actions","s":"Shareholder and member derivative suits; not summarized individually.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"641–647","t":"Joinder of needed parties","s":"Mirrors FRCP 19: a person must be joined when complete relief cannot otherwise be given or the person’s interest would be impaired or others exposed to multiple obligations (art. 641); if joinder is not feasible the court weighs listed factors (art. 642). Nonjoinder is raised by peremptory exception or noticed by the court on its own (arts. 645, 927).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"681–700","t":"Parties plaintiff; procedural capacity","s":"Only a person with a real and actual interest may sue (art. 681); lack of interest is the peremptory exception of no right of action (art. 927). Competent majors and emancipated minors have capacity (art. 682); representatives sue for minors, interdicts, successions, and entities (arts. 683–700). A foreign corporation or LLC may sue in its own name, subject to art. 692 (art. 691).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"731–743","t":"Parties defendant","s":"Rules on who is sued for minors, successions, partnerships, corporations, insurers in receivership, and trusts; not summarized individually.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"801–807","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"When a party dies and the action survives, the legal successor may be substituted on ex parte motion with proof of quality (art. 801), or another party may obtain a summons ordering the successor to appear and substitute (art. 802).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK II, TITLE I","t":"Ordinary Proceedings: Pleading (arts. 851–1156)","s":"Pleadings, petition, exceptions, motions, answer, incidental demands, amendment.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"851–852","t":"Modes of procedure; pleadings allowed","s":"Three modes: ordinary, summary, and executory; Book II governs ordinary proceedings in district courts (art. 851). The only pleadings are petitions, exceptions, written motions, and answers; replicatory pleadings are prohibited and new matter in an exception, contradictory motion, or answer is considered denied (art. 852).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"853–865","t":"Form and signing of pleadings","s":"Caption, numbered paragraphs, adoption by reference (arts. 853–854). Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (art. 856). A final judgment grants the relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded, except in default judgments (art. 862). Art. 863 is Louisiana’s Rule 11: the signer certifies no improper purpose and legal and factual support; the lawyer must state a physical and email address for service. No sanction applies to an original petition filed within 60 days of prescription and dismissed or transferred within 90 days.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"891–893","t":"Petition; pleading damages","s":"The petition must set out the material facts of the transaction or occurrence and a prayer for relief (art. 891). No specific dollar amount of damages may be pleaded; when an amount matters (jurisdiction, jury right, federal diversity), plead generally that the claim exceeds or is less than the threshold. A specific amount is stricken on motion, with possible fees. Does not apply to suits on conventional obligations, notes, open accounts, support, taxes, or garnishment (art. 893).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"921–934","t":"Exceptions (the Louisiana substitute for Rule 12)","s":"Only three exceptions exist: declinatory, dilatory, and peremptory (art. 922). Declinatory: insufficient citation or service (including failure to request service within 90 days under art. 1201(C)), lis pendens, improper venue, lack of personal jurisdiction; waived unless pleaded (art. 925). Dilatory: prematurity, want of amicable demand, unauthorized summary proceeding, nonconformity with art. 891, vagueness, lack of procedural capacity, improper cumulation, discussion; waived unless pleaded (art. 926). Peremptory: prescription, peremption, res judicata, nonjoinder, no cause of action (including partial), no right of action, discharge in bankruptcy, lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; prescription must be specially pleaded and is not supplied by the court except in default cases (art. 927). Declinatory and dilatory exceptions must be filed together before or in the answer and before any other pleading seeking relief (art. 928(A)); the peremptory exception may be filed any time before submission for decision (art. 928(B)). No evidence may be offered on no cause of action (art. 931). If the defect can be cured, the court must order amendment before dismissing (arts. 932–934).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"961–964","t":"Motions","s":"Applications for orders are by motion, written unless made in open court (art. 961). Ex parte motions are allowed where the mover is clearly entitled without proof; otherwise a contradictory motion or rule to show cause is used (art. 963). The court may strike insufficient demands or defenses or scandalous matter after a hearing (art. 964).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"965","t":"Judgment on the pleadings","s":"Any party may move after the answer is filed, but not so as to delay trial; allegations of fact in the mover’s pleadings not denied are taken as admitted.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"966–969","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A plaintiff may move after the answer; a defendant any time. Only listed documents may support or oppose the motion (pleadings, memoranda, affidavits, depositions, interrogatory answers, certified medical records, certified public documents and insurance policies, authentic acts, written stipulations, admissions). Unless the court extends and all parties agree: motion filed and served at least 65 days before trial; opposition at least 15 days before the hearing; reply at least 5 days before (holidays included); hearing set at least 30 days after filing and 30 days before trial, with 30 days’ notice; judgment at least 20 days before trial. Objections to evidence go in the opposition or reply, not a motion to strike. Burden-shifting tracks Celotex. A partial summary judgment on fewer than all claims is interlocutory under art. 1915(C). Not available in divorce or certain spousal property disputes (art. 969). Stricter, fixed deadlines than FRCP 56.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"970","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"Any party may serve a written offer, stating it is under art. 970, at least 20 days before trial; acceptance within 10 days. If the result is at least 25% worse for the offeree than the offer (or a defense verdict on a defendant’s offer), the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs, excluding attorney fees. Unlike FRCP 68, plaintiffs may also make offers.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"971","t":"Special motion to strike (anti-SLAPP)","s":"Claims arising from acts in furtherance of petition or free-speech rights in connection with a public issue are struck unless the plaintiff establishes a probability of success. File within 90 days of service of the petition (later in the court’s discretion); hearing within 30 days of service unless docket conditions require later; discovery stayed until notice of the ruling, absent good cause. A prevailing party on the motion gets reasonable fees and costs. Not applicable to public-prosecutor enforcement actions.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1001–1006","t":"Answer","s":"Answer due 21 days after service of citation; 30 days if discovery was served with the petition; 15 days after an exception filed before answer is overruled or referred to the merits, or after service of an amended petition ordered on an exception (art. 1001). The defendant may answer any time before a default judgment is signed (art. 1002). Allegations not denied are admitted, except as to damages (art. 1004). Affirmative defenses, including comparative fault, must be pleaded (art. 1005).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1031–1041","t":"Incidental demands: general","s":"Incidental demands are reconvention, cross-claims, intervention, and third-party demands (art. 1031). They may be filed without leave until the answer to the principal demand is filed, and later with leave if they will not delay the main action (art. 1033). An incidental demand not prescribed when the main demand was filed is timely if filed within 90 days of service of the main demand (or of the third-party demand) (art. 1041). Dismissal of the main demand does not affect a previously filed incidental demand (art. 1039).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1061–1066","t":"Reconvention (counterclaim)","s":"A defendant may assert any claim against the plaintiff; claims arising out of the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory and must be asserted, with exceptions for certain divorce and paternity actions and art. 3657 (art. 1061).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1071–1073","t":"Cross-claims","s":"A party may assert a cross-claim against a co-party arising out of the same transaction or occurrence (art. 1071). Unlike reconvention, not compulsory under the text.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1091–1094","t":"Intervention","s":"A third person with an interest may intervene by joining the plaintiff, uniting with the defendant, or opposing both (art. 1091). The intervener accepts the proceedings as found (art. 1094).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1111–1116","t":"Third-party demands","s":"A defendant may bring in any person, including a codefendant, who is a warrantor or may be liable for all or part of the principal demand; the plaintiff may then assert claims against the third-party defendant (art. 1111). Failing to bring in a third party does not forfeit the claim unless the third party proves it had a defense that was lost (art. 1113).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1151–1156","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"The plaintiff may amend without leave before the answer is served; the defendant may amend the answer once within 10 days after service; otherwise leave or written consent is needed. A response to an amended petition is due within the time left on the original or 10 days after service, whichever is longer (art. 1151). Declinatory and dilatory exceptions may be amended only to amplify objections already pleaded (art. 1152). Relation back if the amendment arises from the same conduct, transaction, or occurrence (art. 1153); amendment to conform to evidence (art. 1154).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK II, TITLE II","t":"Citation and Service of Process (arts. 1201–1314)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1201–1203","t":"Citation; 90-day service request","s":"Citation and service are essential; without them all proceedings are absolutely null, except in summary and executory proceedings and certain divorce and Children’s Code matters. Service must be requested on all named defendants within 90 days of filing (or of an amended petition adding a defendant), and the clerk’s service fees must be paid within that period or 30 days after a statement of additional fees, whichever is later. The defendant waives the 90-day requirement unless it files a declinatory exception of insufficient service (or a contradictory motion under art. 1672(C)) (art. 1201). The citation must accompany a certified copy of the petition (art. 1202).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1231–1237","t":"Service on individuals","s":"Personal or domiciliary service, at any time including Sundays and holidays (art. 1231). Domiciliary service is made by leaving process at the dwelling with a person of suitable age and discretion residing there (art. 1234).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1261–1267","t":"Service on entities","s":"Corporations and LLCs are served on a registered agent for service; if there is no agent or the agent cannot be served after due diligence, service may be made on any officer or director (or person so listed with the Secretary of State), on an employee of suitable age and discretion where business is regularly conducted, or under R.S. 13:3204 for out-of-state entities (arts. 1261, 1266); Secretary of State service is in arts. 1262 and 1267.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1291–1293","t":"Who serves","s":"The sheriff of the parish where service is made or where the action is pending serves process (art. 1291) and makes a return that is prima facie correct and may be amended unless material prejudice results (art. 1292). If the sheriff has not served within 10 days, or returns that service failed, the court on motion appoints a private process server (art. 1293). Unlike FRCP 4, private service is not available as of right.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1311–1314","t":"Service of later pleadings","s":"Pleadings after the petition are served by mail, delivery, or email to counsel at the address designated for electronic service, with a certificate of service; mail service is complete on mailing (art. 1313(A)–(B)). A pleading or order that sets a court date must be served by registered or certified mail, commercial courier, email to the designated address with electronic delivery confirmation, or by the sheriff (art. 1313(C)). Pleadings that require citation-level service are served by the sheriff (art. 1314).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK II, TITLE III","t":"Production of Evidence (arts. 1351–1475)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1351–1357","t":"Subpoenas","s":"The clerk issues subpoenas (art. 1351). Louisiana witnesses may be compelled to attend trial anywhere in the state, subject to R.S. 13:3661 (art. 1352). A person served with a subpoena duces tecum may send written objections within 15 days of service, or before the compliance date if sooner (art. 1354). Noncompliance is contempt (art. 1357).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1420–1428","t":"Discovery: general","s":"Signing of discovery requests certifies a reasonable basis (art. 1420). Scope: any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter, reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence (art. 1422) – the older, broader federal standard without FRCP 26(b)(1) proportionality. Insurance agreements are discoverable (art. 1423). Work product is protected unless denial would unfairly prejudice (art. 1424). Expert disclosures, if ordered, are due 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days of the other side’s disclosure); a motion challenging an expert’s qualifications or methodology must be filed 60 days before trial and decided 30 days before trial (art. 1425). Protective orders (art. 1426). No automatic initial disclosures appear in these articles.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1429–1456","t":"Depositions","s":"Any party may depose any person after suit is filed; the plaintiff needs leave to depose within 15 days after service of citation unless an exception applies (art. 1437). Reasonable written notice is required (art. 1438). Organizational depositions mirror FRCP 30(b)(6) (art. 1442). An expert’s deposition may be used for any purpose on notice, but any counsel may object within 10 days and require live testimony (art. 1450(A)(5)).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1457–1460","t":"Interrogatories","s":"Limit of 35 including subparts; 35 more allowed on ex parte motion; beyond that, only on contradictory motion for good cause (art. 1457). Answers due within 30 days of service (15 days in family law cases unless served with the petition) (art. 1458). Option to produce business records (art. 1460).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1461–1465.1","t":"Production; medical examinations","s":"Requests for production and entry on land (art. 1461); written response due within 30 days of service (15 days in family law cases unless served with the petition) (art. 1462). The court may order an additional medical opinion (physical or mental examination) when condition is in controversy (art. 1464).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1466–1468","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff after filing and on others with or after service of the petition (art. 1466). Answers must specifically admit, deny, or explain; the requester may test sufficiency, and before a court orders a request deemed admitted (except in a default) a Rule 10.1 conference certificate is required (art. 1467, as amended by Acts 2026, No. 942). The collected text of art. 1467 states no fixed answer period; check the current article and local practice. Admitted matters are conclusively established unless withdrawal is allowed (art. 1468).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1469–1475","t":"Compelling discovery; sanctions","s":"Motion to compel and expenses (art. 1469); refusal after an order is contempt (art. 1470); sanctions for disobeying an order include establishing facts, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, and fees (art. 1471); expenses for failing to admit what is later proved (art. 1472); sanctions for failing to attend a deposition or respond (art. 1473). Before moving to compel, District Court Rule 10.1 requires a conference and a certificate.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK II, TITLES IV–V","t":"Pretrial, Trial, Dismissal, Default, Jury (arts. 1551–1814)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1551–1552","t":"Pretrial and scheduling conference","s":"The court may, in its discretion, call conferences (in chambers, by phone, or by video) to set deadlines for art. 1425(F), summary judgment, and other motions, set trial and the jury-bond deadline, simplify issues, and rule on exhibits. If a suit has been pending more than one year after service on all defendants with no trial date, on any party’s motion the court must set a conference to schedule discovery and trial (art. 1551).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1561–1563","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Consolidation for trial or limited purposes (art. 1561); separate trial of insurance coverage, liability, or damages issues (art. 1562).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1571–1573","t":"Assignment for trial","s":"District courts set trial-assignment procedures by rule (art. 1571). Notice of trial is sent under art. 1313(A)(4) or by certified mail by the clerk at least 10 days before trial (art. 1572). Scheduling preference for a party age 70 or older or with a documented illness making survival beyond six months unlikely (art. 1573).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1601–1605","t":"Continuances","s":"Discretionary for good ground (art. 1601); mandatory if, despite due diligence, a party cannot obtain material evidence or a material witness is absent without the party’s contrivance (art. 1602).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1631–1637","t":"Trial procedure","s":"Court controls the proceedings and may exclude witnesses (art. 1631); order of trial (art. 1632); a party or person identified with a party may be called and examined as on cross (art. 1634); formal exceptions to rulings are unnecessary (art. 1635); proffer of excluded evidence (art. 1636); art. 1633.1 addresses live trial testimony by video.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1671–1673","t":"Dismissal","s":"Voluntary dismissal without prejudice on the plaintiff’s application and payment of costs if made before any appearance by the defendant; afterward, the court may refuse to dismiss except with prejudice (art. 1671). Involuntary dismissal when the plaintiff fails to appear for trial; if all parties fail to appear and a settlement is pending, either may reinstate within 60 days of notice of dismissal (art. 1672(A)). In a bench trial, any party may move for dismissal after the plaintiff rests (art. 1672(B)). Art. 1672(C) provides for dismissal without prejudice of a named defendant for whom service was not requested within the art. 1201(C) period. Dismissal with prejudice has the effect of a final judgment after trial (art. 1673).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1702–1704","t":"Default judgment","s":"There is no preliminary default: if the defendant fails to answer timely, the plaintiff may obtain a default judgment by proving a prima facie case with competent, admissible evidence. Notice of intent to take a default is required at least 7 days before judgment when the defendant has appeared of record (certified mail or delivery to counsel), when the defendant’s lawyer has written to the plaintiff about the case, and in tort cases (regular mail to the service address) (art. 1702(A)). Defaults may be confirmed without a hearing on a written submission meeting art. 1702.1. Defaults against the state or a political subdivision require notice to the attorney general or agent and 21 days to answer (art. 1704). A default judgment may not differ in kind from what the petition demanded, and damages are the amount proven (art. 1703).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1731–1736","t":"Jury trial","s":"No jury where no individual plaintiff’s claim exceeds $10,000 exclusive of interest and costs, with rules on stipulations made more or less than 60 days before trial (art. 1732(1)); art. 1732 lists other non-jury cases. Demand a jury by pleading within 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to a jury issue, and post the bond set by the court (art. 1733(A)(1), (C)). In tort cases where the plaintiff stipulates the claim is over $10,000 and under $50,000, the jury demand requires a $5,000 cash deposit within 60 days of the request or the jury is waived (art. 1733(A)(2)). The bond deadline is no later than 60 days before trial; if not filed, any other party has 10 more days to file it (art. 1734). Unlike FRCP 38, a bond or deposit for jury costs is required.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1751–1769","t":"Jury selection","s":"The court examines prospective jurors and the parties or lawyers may then examine them individually (art. 1763). Peremptory challenges: 3 per side for a jury of six (court may add up to 2 with multiple parties on a side), 6 per side for a jury of twelve (up to 4 more); each side gets an equal number (art. 1764). Challenges for cause (art. 1765); method and timing (art. 1766).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1791–1797","t":"Jury instructions and verdict","s":"The judge may not comment on the facts (art. 1791). Written requests for instructions at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court informs the parties of its proposed instructions, and an instruction may be assigned as error only if the party objected before or immediately after the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds (art. 1793). Non-unanimous verdicts: 5 of 6 or 9 of 12 jurors must concur unless the parties stipulate otherwise (art. 1797).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1810–1814","t":"Directed verdict, JNOV, verdict forms, remittitur","s":"A directed-verdict motion does not waive the right to present evidence (art. 1810). A motion for JNOV is due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers in open court, or the sheriff serves, the notice of judgment; a new-trial motion after a JNOV ruling is due within 7 days (exclusive of holidays) after notice of the JNOV judgment (art. 1811). Special verdicts and general verdicts with interrogatories (arts. 1812–1813). Remittitur or additur as an alternative to new trial (art. 1814).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK II, TITLE VI","t":"Judgments (arts. 1841–2031)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1841","t":"Final and interlocutory judgments","s":"As amended in 2026: a judgment that determines the merits in whole is final; one that determines the merits in part is either a partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) or an interlocutory judgment under art. 1915(C); one that decides only preliminary matters is interlocutory.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1871–1883","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Courts may declare rights, status, and legal relations whether or not further relief is claimed (art. 1871); the court may refuse a declaration in the circumstances stated in art. 1876 (title only collected). Art. 1878 addresses supplemental relief and an expedited hearing for constitutional challenges (per its title; text not collected).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1911–1913","t":"Signing and notice of final judgment","s":"No appeal lies from a final judgment until it is signed by the judge (art. 1911(B)). The clerk must mail (or deliver in open court) notice of signing of a final judgment in contested cases to counsel and unrepresented parties and certify the date; a default judgment against a defendant not personally served who never appeared must be served by the sheriff (art. 1913). Appeal and new-trial delays run from this notice.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1914","t":"Interlocutory judgments; notice","s":"Rendition of an interlocutory judgment in open court is notice, unless the court orders it written, a party asks within 10 days that it be reduced to writing, the court took it under advisement, or it is a partial judgment under art. 1915(C), in which case the clerk sends notice. Each party then has 10 days to act in the trial court; this does not change writ or appeal deadlines. Notice of denial of a new trial or JNOV must be mailed, and the appeal delay runs from that mailing.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1915","t":"Partial final judgments","s":"A partial judgment is final (and appealable) only in the listed situations: dismissal of fewer than all parties; judgment on the pleadings; summary judgment (other than one on a particular issue under art. 966(E)); judgment on a separately tried principal or incidental demand; a separately tried liability judgment; and certain sanctions. As amended by Acts 2025, No. 250, a judgment, summary judgment, or sustained exception that disposes of fewer than all claims, demands, issues, or theories by or against a party is interlocutory; the collected text contains no procedure for the trial court to designate it final. Differs sharply from FRCP 54(b).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1916–1918","t":"Preparing and form of judgment","s":"After a jury verdict, the court signs a judgment within 10 days or orders counsel to submit one (art. 1916). In appealable non-jury contested cases, written findings and reasons must be given if requested within 10 days after mailing of notice of judgment (art. 1917(A)). A final judgment must be identified as final, signed and dated, and name who wins, who loses, and the relief; reasons go in a separate opinion; a judgment lacking decretal language is remanded for amendment (art. 1918).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1920–1922","t":"Costs; interest","s":"Costs are paid by the party cast unless the judgment says otherwise, and the court may assess them as it considers equitable (art. 1920). Interest is awarded as prayed for or as provided by law (art. 1921).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1951","t":"Amendment of judgment","s":"A final judgment may be amended at any time to alter phraseology, fix deficient decretal language, or correct calculation errors, but not to change substance; amendment requires a hearing with notice unless all parties consent or the submitter certifies the amendment was circulated at least 5 days earlier without opposition.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"1971–1979","t":"New trial","s":"Peremptory grounds: verdict or judgment clearly contrary to the law and evidence; newly discovered evidence that could not have been found with due diligence; jury bribed or misbehaved (art. 1972). Discretionary grounds: any good ground (art. 1973). Motion due within 7 days, exclusive of legal holidays, after the clerk mails or delivers notice of judgment or the sheriff serves it (art. 1974). Facts for grounds (2) and (3) must be verified by affidavit (art. 1975). The court must decide within 10 days after submission unless all counsel stipulate otherwise, and must state its reasons when granting (art. 1979).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2001–2006","t":"Action of nullity","s":"A judgment rendered against an unrepresented incompetent, against a defendant not served and who did not waive, or by a court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction is annulled; the action may be brought any time, unless the defendant acquiesced (arts. 2002–2003). A judgment obtained by fraud or ill practices may be annulled by an action filed within one year of discovery, with possible fees (art. 2004). Nullity actions are brought in the trial court (art. 2006).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2031","t":"Revival of judgments","s":"A money judgment may be revived before it prescribes by ex parte motion in the same suit; filing the motion interrupts prescription. (The 10-year prescription of money judgments is in Civil Code art. 3501, not collected.)","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK III","t":"Proceedings in Appellate Courts (arts. 2081–2201)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2081–2086","t":"Appealable judgments; who may appeal","s":"Final judgments are appealable (art. 2083(A)). A partial final judgment under art. 1915(A) is appealable; other partial judgments only when a law expressly says so; an interlocutory judgment only when a law expressly provides (art. 2083(B)–(C), as amended 2026). A party who confessed judgment or voluntarily and unconditionally acquiesced in it cannot appeal (art. 2085). A person who could have intervened may appeal (art. 2086).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2087","t":"Devolutive appeal: 60 days","s":"A devolutive appeal (one that does not suspend execution) is taken within 60 days of (1) expiry of the delay for a new-trial or JNOV motion if none was timely filed, or (2) mailing of notice of denial of a timely new-trial or JNOV motion. An appellee seeking to change the judgment may take its own devolutive appeal within those 60 days or 10 days after the clerk mails notice of the first appeal, whichever is later. When several parties move for new trial or JNOV, delays run for all from the ruling on the last motion. An order of appeal granted before all such motions are decided is premature but becomes effective on their denial. Removal to federal court interrupts the delay, which starts anew on remand.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2088","t":"Trial court loses jurisdiction","s":"Jurisdiction over matters reviewable on appeal passes to the appellate court when the order of appeal is granted (devolutive) or when the order is granted and the bond timely filed (suspensive). The trial court keeps listed powers, such as extending the return day, correcting the record, testing the bond, granting appeals to others, executing an unsuspended judgment, taxing costs and fees, and amending decretal language. If a suspensive bond is not timely filed, the trial court may convert the appeal to devolutive, except in eviction cases.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2121–2122","t":"How to appeal: order of appeal","s":"An appeal is taken by obtaining an order of appeal from the trial court that rendered the judgment, on oral motion in open court, written motion, or petition, within the delay. The order sets the return day and any security. The clerk mails notice of appeal to other parties and the appellate court; the clerk’s failure does not void the appeal (art. 2121). Unlike FRAP 3, there is no simple notice of appeal filed as of right; you must move for and obtain an order.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2123–2124","t":"Suspensive appeal: 30 days plus security","s":"A suspensive appeal (one that stops execution) must be taken, and the security furnished, within 30 days of the same triggers as art. 2087 (art. 2123). No security is required for a devolutive appeal. For a money judgment, suspensive-appeal security equals the judgment plus interest to the date furnished, excluding costs; for judgments over $150 million (outside listed exceptions) the court may set a lower amount; for good cause the court may require up to 150% when a surety bond is used; supervisory review of the amount interrupts the delay (art. 2124).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2125–2128.1","t":"Return day, costs, record","s":"The return day is 30 days after estimated costs are paid (45 days if testimony must be transcribed), unless the judge sets a shorter time; the trial court may grant one extension of up to 30 days, and further extensions come from the appellate court (art. 2125). The appellant must pay the clerk’s estimated record costs within 20 days after notice (one 20-day extension for good cause); if not paid, the trial judge after a hearing dismisses the appeal as abandoned or allows 10 more days (arts. 2126, 2088(A)(9)). The clerk prepares and lodges the record (art. 2127). The appellant may designate a partial record within 10 days after the cost notice, and the appellee may counter-designate within 14 days; absent designation the whole record goes up (art. 2128).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2129–2132","t":"Assignments of error; narrative of facts","s":"No assignment of errors is needed in the trial court, but if the appellant designates a partial record it must serve a concise statement of points relied on and is limited to them (art. 2129). If testimony was not transcribed, the parties prepare a narrative of facts or the judge makes one (arts. 2130–2131). The trial court may correct the record (art. 2132).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2133","t":"Answer to appeal (cross-appeal substitute)","s":"An appellee who wants the judgment changed, or wants damages for frivolous appeal, must file an answer to the appeal stating the relief within 15 days after the return day or the lodging of the record, whichever is later; it operates as an appeal. Other parties to an incidental demand then have 15 days to file similar answers. An appellee not seeking change may defend the judgment on any ground supported by the record without appealing.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2161–2168","t":"Procedure in the appellate court","s":"An appeal is not dismissed for record defects; motions to dismiss for irregularities imputable to the appellant must be filed within 3 days (exclusive of holidays) after the return day or lodging, whichever is later (art. 2161). An appeal may be dismissed any time by consent, for lack of jurisdiction or of a right to appeal, or as abandoned; a misdirected appeal may be transferred (art. 2162). A peremptory exception may be filed first in the appellate court before submission if proof appears of record, and on prescription or peremption the plaintiff may demand a remand to try it (art. 2163). The appellate court renders any judgment that is just, legal, and proper on the record and may award damages and fees for a frivolous appeal or writ application (art. 2164). Rehearing in a court of appeal is due within 14 days of transmission of notice of judgment; certiorari to the Supreme Court within 30 days of that transmission, or 30 days after notice of denial of rehearing (art. 2166). Supreme Court rehearing: 14 days (art. 2167). Unpublished opinions are posted online and may be cited (art. 2168).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2201","t":"Supervisory writs","s":"Supervisory writs are applied for and granted under the constitution and the rules of the Supreme Court and the courts of appeal (see Uniform Rules of the Courts of Appeal, Rule 4: notice of intent and a return date not exceeding 30 days from notice of the ruling under art. 1914).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK IV","t":"Execution of Judgments (arts. 2251–2789)","s":"Selected articles.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2251–2254","t":"Execution generally","s":"Execution is only in the trial court (art. 2251). A creditor may execute only after the suspensive-appeal delay has run; recording the judgment earlier is not execution (art. 2252). The clerk issues a writ to the sheriff on the creditor’s request (art. 2253).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2291–2299","t":"Fieri facias","s":"A money judgment is executed by writ of fieri facias directing seizure and sale of the debtor’s property (art. 2291).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2411–2417","t":"Garnishment","s":"After a writ of fieri facias issues, the creditor may cite a garnishee to declare under oath what property of the debtor it holds (art. 2411). The garnishee must file sworn answers to interrogatories within 30 days of service, and failure may make the garnishee personally liable for the whole debt; the creditor must notify the debtor of the garnishment petition (art. 2412).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2451–2456","t":"Judgment-debtor examination","s":"In aid of execution, the creditor may examine the debtor and the debtor’s books and documents about the debtor’s property (art. 2451).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2541–2542","t":"Foreign judgments","s":"A judgment of a federal court, another state, or a foreign country may be recognized in an ordinary proceeding against the debtor with an authenticated copy attached; judgments entitled to full faith and credit may also be enforced under the Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act, R.S. 13:4241 (art. 2541). Art. 2542 lists when a foreign-country defamation judgment is not conclusive (no impartial tribunals or due process, no personal or subject-matter jurisdiction) and other grounds on which it need not be recognized.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK V","t":"Summary and Executory Proceedings (arts. 2591–2814)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2591–2596","t":"Summary proceedings","s":"Conducted with rapidity, without citation and the formalities of ordinary proceedings (art. 2591). Allowed only for listed matters, including incidental questions and attorney-fee awards, new-trial motions, issues raised by exception or rule, habeas, mandamus, quo warranto, custody, support, and injunctive relief (art. 2592). Begun by contradictory motion or rule to show cause; exceptions must be filed before the trial (art. 2593); no citation is needed (art. 2594); tried by preference and without a jury (art. 2595).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2631–2724","t":"Executory proceedings","s":"Seizure and sale of mortgaged property without prior citation or judgment, to enforce a mortgage or privilege in an authentic act importing a confession of judgment (arts. 2631–2632). A suspensive appeal from the order of seizure and sale must be taken within 15 days of service of the notice of seizure (art. 2642). The debtor may enjoin the sale on listed grounds such as extinguishment of the debt (art. 2751).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"2781–2783","t":"Making Louisiana judgments executory elsewhere","s":"A judgment of one Louisiana court may be made executory in another Louisiana court of competent jurisdiction (art. 2781).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOKS VI–VII","t":"Probate and Other Special Proceedings (arts. 2811–4736)","s":"Successions, tutorship, interdiction, partition, concursus, and eviction were not summarized except as noted.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"3601–3613","t":"Injunctions","s":"Injunctions issue where irreparable injury may otherwise result or as provided by law (art. 3601). A preliminary injunction is heard 2 to 10 days after service of notice (art. 3602). A TRO without notice requires specific facts in a verified petition, affidavit, or affirmation showing immediate and irreparable injury before the other side can be heard, plus the attorney’s written certification of efforts to give notice or reasons notice should not be required (art. 3603). A TRO expires within the time set, not more than 10 days, and may be extended for good cause for periods of up to 10 days each (art. 3604). A TRO or preliminary injunction may be dissolved or modified on 2 days’ notice (art. 3607); damages and fees for wrongful issuance (art. 3608); supporting affidavits must be delivered at least 24 hours before the hearing (art. 3609); security is required unless dispensed with by law (art. 3610). No appeal lies from a TRO order; an appeal from a preliminary-injunction order must be taken, and any bond furnished, within 15 days, and does not suspend the order unless the court so orders (art. 3612).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"3751–3753","t":"Notice of pendency (lis pendens recordation)","s":"A suit affecting title to, or asserting a mortgage or privilege on, immovable property is not notice to third persons unless a notice of pendency is made and filed or recorded as art. 3752 requires (art. 3751).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"3781–3866","t":"Extraordinary remedies","s":"Habeas corpus, mandamus, and quo warranto are ordered only on petition and may be tried summarily (art. 3781). Mandamus directs a public officer, a corporation or its officer, or an LLC or its member or manager to perform duties listed in arts. 3863–3864, where the law gives no ordinary relief or delay would cause injustice, but not to compel state spending certified as causing a deficit (arts. 3861–3862).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"4701–4736","t":"Eviction","s":"Notice to vacate gives the lessee at least 5 days from delivery (art. 4701). After a rule to show cause, a judgment of eviction issues (art. 4732). An appeal does not suspend eviction unless the defendant answered under oath with an affirmative defense and applied for appeal and filed bond within 24 hours of the judgment (art. 4735).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK VIII","t":"Trial Courts of Limited Jurisdiction (arts. 4831–5003)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"4831–4852","t":"Jurisdiction and venue","s":"Parish courts: concurrent with district courts where the amount in dispute does not exceed $20,000 (art. 4842). City courts: generally $15,000, with higher limits for listed city courts in art. 4843(D) and beyond, and unlimited jurisdiction over certain ordinance-enforcement suits by public bodies in larger cities (art. 4843).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"4871–4875","t":"No jury; transfer to district court","s":"No jury trial in parish, city, or justice of the peace courts (art. 4871). A defendant entitled to a jury may transfer the case to district court under art. 4873 (art. 4872).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"4901–4925","t":"Procedure in limited-jurisdiction courts","s":"Special pleading, answer, default, and new-trial delays apply (arts. 4901–4908, 4911–4923; headings only, texts not collected). Appeals from justice of the peace courts go to the parish court or, if none, the district court, by suit for trial de novo filed within 15 days of judgment or notice (arts. 4924–4925).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5001–5003","t":"Appeals from city and parish courts","s":"Appeal is to the court of appeal on the record (art. 5001), and must be taken within 10 days of the judgment or of service of notice of judgment when notice is required (art. 5002). Much shorter than the district-court delays.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"BOOK IX","t":"Miscellaneous Provisions and Definitions (arts. 5051–5251)","s":"","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5051–5058","t":"Construction","s":"The Code is construed liberally, with regard for the fact that procedure implements substantive law and is not an end in itself (art. 5051). “Shall” is mandatory and “may” permissive (art. 5053).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5059","t":"Computing time","s":"Exclude the day of the triggering event; include the last day unless it is a legal holiday, then run to the next non-holiday. Legal holidays (including half-holidays) count, except when expressly excluded, when the holiday is the last day, or when the period is less than 7 days. Note that several key delays (new trial, JNOV) say “exclusive of legal holidays” expressly.","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5091–5098","t":"Curator ad hoc for absent defendants","s":"On the plaintiff’s petition or ex parte motion, the court appoints an attorney (curator ad hoc) to represent a defendant over whom it has jurisdiction who is a nonresident or absentee not served and not waiving, an unrepresented minor or incompetent, or deceased without a succession representative, and in listed in rem cases (art. 5091). The appointee’s duties are in arts. 5094–5095 (not collected).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5121–5127","t":"Judicial bonds","s":"Rules on bonds payable to the clerk, sureties, and testing sufficiency (headings only; texts not collected).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5181–5188","t":"Proceeding without prepayment of costs (in forma pauperis)","s":"An individual unable to pay court costs because of poverty may sue or defend in any trial or appellate court without paying costs in advance or furnishing security; incarcerated felons must advance costs on a statutory schedule (art. 5181). Affidavits and traverse are in arts. 5183–5184 (not collected).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"},{"n":"5251","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines terms used throughout the Code (not collected).","u":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Laws_Toc.aspx?folder=68&level=Parent"}]});
